## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design multi-tenant data contracts, runtime validation, privacy masking, and governance in a real-time setting. Also probes trade-offs between Spark and Flink, plus testing strategies and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime data contracts per tenant
- Per-tenant masking/redaction strategies
- GDPR deletion and data sovereignty
- Schema evolution in lakehouse (Iceberg/Parquet)
- Policy engine governance (e.g., OPA)
- Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink trade-offs
- Testing, monitoring, data quality

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo runtime contract check
function validateRecord(record, contract) {
  for (const field of Object.keys(contract.fields)) {
    if (!record.hasOwnProperty(field) && contract.fields[field].required) {
      throw new Error(`Missing required field ${field}`);
    }
  }
  // redaction example
  if (contract.mask) {
    for (const f of contract.mask) {
      if (record[f]) record[f] = "REDACTED";
    }
  }
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant masking guarantees in CI/CD?
- How would GDPR deletion requests propagate through streaming pipelines and downstream consumers?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data quality, late data handling, and observability in a real-world lakehouse pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and simple validator logic
- Upserts into Iceberg star schema with deduplication by event_id
- Late data handling with watermarking (15 minutes)
- Lightweight observability: lineage, conforming vs non-conforming counts, metrics sink
- Trade-offs: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink for beginner workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator
function validate(record) {
  if (!record.event_id || !record.ts || !record.device_id) return false;
  if (typeof record.metrics?.value !== 'number') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design the bad_data retention and purge policy?
- Which metrics would you expose to operators for quick health checks?

## Why This Is Asked

Data governance for multiple data sources with contracts ensures safety, compatibility, and faster onboarding of teams at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and schema registry
- Versioned Iceberg schemas and per-source isolation
- Idempotent upserts and SCD2 history
- Contract testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-contract check: validate record against contract version
function validate(record, contract) {
  // check required fields
  for (const f of contract.required) {
    if (!(f in record)) return false;
  }
  // type checks
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(contract.types)) {
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect contract drift across sources, and what actions would you trigger?
- How would you handle data already ingested when a contract is updated—rollback, versioning, or retroactive validation?

## Why This Is Asked
Design a scalable, auditable CDC ingestion that evolves schemas safely while preserving privacy and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Central schema registry with versioned contracts
- Idempotent upserts and MERGE semantics
- Dynamic schema evolution and tenant isolation
- Late data buffering and privacy-preserving BI views
- End-to-end testing and monitoring of contracts and lineage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: contract validation and upsert
function upsertEvent(event, registry) {
  const contract = registry.getContract(event.version, event.schemaKey);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  // perform idempotent merge into Iceberg
  mergeIntoIceberg(event, contract.primaryKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift in production and implement safe rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant data isolation and lineage end-to-end?
